CUDA decode mat-vecs read 16 bytes per load (row_dot): bf16 gemv, mxfp8/mxfp4/nvfp4
quantized_matmul and the fp expert gather with fewer than 8 rows per expert. Short-N rows split
over up to 256 threads, and each weight load is shared by up to 8 rows of x. device_info reports
`integrated`. The arithmetic is in mlx/backend/cuda/device/row_dot_ops.cuh, a JIT header, so a
cuda_kernel that must match it bit for bit (mlx-serve's moe_cuda.zig) includes the same code.
The one-row fp quantized_matmul arm runs on integrated GPUs only: on a discrete RTX 5060 Ti the
stock kernel is faster. fp4 decodes through the cvt instruction where the target has it
(sm_120a, sm_121a) and through an exact byte-permute table elsewhere (plain sm_120 emulated it).
The fp modes take the mat-vec through 8 rows on GB10, where one weight read beats the
tiled kernel (MLX_CUDA_QMV_MAX_ROWS still decides).

Retires when upstream MLX's CUDA gemv and quantized mat-vecs read 16-byte vectors for these
formats at decode widths. Not reported upstream yet.

